---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/page.css';

const navigation = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'requirements', label: 'Requirements' },
  { id: 'installation', label: 'Installation' },
  { id: 'development', label: 'Development' },
  { id: 'architecture', label: 'Architecture' },
  { id: 'safety', label: 'Detection safety' },
  { id: 'testing', label: 'Testing' },
  { id: 'debugging', label: 'Debugging' },
  { id: 'verification', label: 'Manual verification' },
  { id: 'troubleshooting', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
  { id: 'limitations', label: 'Limitations' }
];

---

<MainLayout
  title="Documentation"
  description="Technical documentation for YesYouTube, including installation, architecture, testing and debugging."
>

</MainLayout>

  <section class="docs-hero yy-section-sm">
    <div class="yy-container">

      <span class="eyebrow">Developer documentation</span>

      <h1 class="mt-3">Documentation.</h1>

      <p>
        Everything you need to install, understand, build,
        test and troubleshoot YesYouTube.
      </p>

      <div class="docs-hero-meta">
        <span class="yy-badge">Manifest V3</span>
        <span class="yy-badge">TypeScript</span>
        <span class="yy-badge">Open source</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-layout yy-section-sm">
    <div class="yy-container">
      <div class="row gy-5">
        <aside class="col-lg-3">

          <nav
            class="docs-sidebar"
            aria-label="Documentation navigation"
          >

            <span class="docs-sidebar-title">
              ON THIS PAGE
            </span>

            {navigation.map((item) => (
              <a
                href={`#${item.id}`}
                class="docs-nav-link"
              >
                {item.label}
              </a>
            ))}

          </nav>

        </aside>

        <div class="col-lg-9 docs-content">
        <section id="overview" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">01 / Overview</span>

            <h2>What is YesYouTube?</h2>

            <p>
              YesYouTube is a Manifest V3 browser extension written
              in TypeScript. It detects YouTube's inactivity
              confirmation prompt and automatically clicks its
              valid confirmation button.
            </p>

            <p>
              Its purpose is simple: reduce interruptions during
              long viewing sessions without requiring manual
              interaction with the confirmation dialog.
            </p>

            <p>
              The extension operates through a single content script.
              It has no background service worker, makes no network
              requests and declares no additional extension permissions.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-highlight">

              <span class="eyebrow">Supported platforms</span>

              <div class="docs-platforms">
                <span>▶ YouTube</span>
                <span>♫ YouTube Music</span>
              </div>

              <p>
                Supported pages are limited to
                www.youtube.com and music.youtube.com.
              </p>

            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="requirements" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">02 / Requirements</span>

            <h2>What you'll need.</h2>

            <p>
              Before building YesYouTube locally, make sure
              your development environment meets these requirements.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-requirements">

              <div class="docs-requirement">

                <div class="requirement-icon">⌘</div>

                <div>
                  <h3>Node.js</h3>
                  <p>Version 20 or newer.</p>
                </div>

              </div>

              <div class="docs-requirement">

                <div class="requirement-icon">npm</div>

                <div>
                  <h3>npm</h3>
                  <p>Version 10 or newer.</p>
                </div>

              </div>

              <div class="docs-requirement">

                <div class="requirement-icon">◈</div>

                <div>
                  <h3>Manifest V3 browser</h3>
                  <p>Google Chrome or Microsoft Edge.</p>
                </div>

              </div>

            </div>

          </section>


          <section id="installation" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">03 / Installation</span>

            <h2>Get started.</h2>

            <p>
              Build the extension locally and load the generated
              distribution directory into your browser.
            </p>


            <h3>Step 01 — Clone the repository</h3>

            <p>
              Open your terminal and clone the YesYouTube project.
            </p>

            <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YesYouTube.git
cd YesYouTube</code></pre>


            <h3>Step 02 — Install dependencies</h3>

            <pre><code>npm install</code></pre>


            <h3>Step 03 — Build the extension</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run build</code></pre>

            <p>
              The production build generates the extension files
              inside the dist directory.
            </p>


            <div class="docs-install-card">

              <div class="install-card-heading">
                <span class="eyebrow">Chrome installation</span>
                <span class="install-browser">Chrome</span>
              </div>

              <ol>
                <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions/</code>.</li>
                <li>Enable Developer mode.</li>
                <li>Click Load unpacked.</li>
                <li>Select the generated <code>dist/</code> directory.</li>
                <li>Open YouTube in a browser tab.</li>
              </ol>

            </div>


            <div class="docs-install-card">

              <div class="install-card-heading">
                <span class="eyebrow">Edge installation</span>
                <span class="install-browser">Edge</span>
              </div>

              <ol>
                <li>Open <code>edge://extensions/</code>.</li>
                <li>Enable Developer mode.</li>
                <li>Click Load unpacked.</li>
                <li>Select the generated <code>dist/</code> directory.</li>
                <li>Open YouTube in a browser tab.</li>
              </ol>

            </div>


            <div class="docs-notice">

              <strong>After making changes:</strong>

              Rebuild the extension, click Reload on its browser
              extension card, and refresh the YouTube tab.

            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="development" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">04 / Development</span>

            <h2>Development commands.</h2>

            <p>
              YesYouTube uses TypeScript, esbuild and Vitest
              for its development and build workflow.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-command-list">

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm install</code>
                <span>Install dependencies.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run typecheck</code>
                <span>Run strict TypeScript checks.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm test</code>
                <span>Run automated tests.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run test:watch</code>
                <span>Run tests in watch mode.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run build</code>
                <span>Create a production build.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run build:debug</code>
                <span>Create a debug build.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run watch</code>
                <span>Rebuild on source changes.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-command-item">
                <code>npm run clean</code>
                <span>Remove generated dist files.</span>
              </div>

            </div>

            <h3>Production build</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run build</code></pre>

            <p>
              Generates the production extension inside dist/.
              Debugging interfaces and diagnostic logging are
              removed from production bundles.
            </p>

            <h3>Debug build</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run build:debug</code></pre>

            <p>
              Enables diagnostic logging and exposes
              window.youtubeAutoContinue.
            </p>

            <h3>Watch mode</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run watch</code></pre>

            <p>
              Rebuilds the extension whenever source files change.
              Watch mode uses the debug build.
            </p>

          </section>

          <section id="architecture" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">05 / Architecture</span>

            <h2>Small by design.</h2>

            <p>
              YesYouTube separates DOM matching from lifecycle
              management and click decisions. Each module has
              one clearly defined responsibility.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-module-list">

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">01</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>types.ts</code>
                  <p>
                    Defines DetectorConfig and DetectionResult
                    contracts. Contains no side effects.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">02</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>config.ts</code>
                  <p>
                    Centralizes selectors, prompt patterns
                    and confirmation labels.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">03</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>matcher.ts</code>
                  <p>
                    Scans the DOM and returns matching results.
                    It identifies candidates but never clicks.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">04</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>detector.ts</code>
                  <p>
                    Owns observers, timers, listeners,
                    lifecycle and the click decision.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">05</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>index.ts</code>
                  <p>
                    Entry point that initializes the content script.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-module-item">
                <div class="module-index">06</div>
                <div class="module-information">
                  <code>src/test/</code>
                  <p>
                    Contains simulated DOM fixtures and automated tests.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

            </div>

            <h3>Directory structure</h3>

            <pre><code>src/
├── manifest.json
├── content/
│   ├── types.ts
│   ├── config.ts
│   ├── matcher.ts
│   ├── detector.ts
│   └── index.ts
└── test/
    ├── simulate.ts
    ├── matcher.test.ts
    └── detector.test.ts

scripts/
├── build.ts
└── generate-icons.ts

vitest.config.mts
tsconfig.json
package.json</code></pre>

            <h3>Module boundaries</h3>

            <ul>
              <li><code>matcher.ts</code> never performs clicks.</li>
              <li><code>detector.ts</code> owns stateful behaviour.</li>
              <li><code>config.ts</code> centralizes detection rules.</li>
              <li><code>index.ts</code> initializes the content script.</li>
              <li>Test fixtures never enter the production bundle.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Design principle:</strong>
              Keep detection, decision-making and lifecycle
              management separate and independently testable.
            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="safety" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">06 / Detection safety</span>

            <h2>Four gates before every click.</h2>

            <p>
              The detector follows a restrictive approach:
              a confirmation button is considered only after
              its containing dialog has been identified.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-safety-list">

              <div class="docs-safety-item">
                <span>01</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>Dialog identification</h3>
                  <p>A configured dialog selector must match.</p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-safety-item">
                <span>02</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>Visibility verification</h3>
                  <p>
                    The dialog and its ancestors must be visible,
                    not hidden or aria-hidden.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-safety-item">
                <span>03</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>Prompt text matching</h3>
                  <p>
                    Dialog text must be short and match a
                    configured inactivity prompt pattern.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-safety-item">
                <span>04</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>Confirmation verification</h3>
                  <p>
                    A visible, enabled button inside that dialog
                    must have an exact configured confirmation label.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

            </div>

            <h3>Accepted confirmation labels</h3>

            <div class="docs-label-list">
              <span>Yes</span>
              <span>OK</span>
              <span>Continue</span>
              <span>Continue watching</span>
            </div>

            <h3>What gets ignored?</h3>

            <ul>
              <li>Unrelated dialogs containing Yes buttons.</li>
              <li>Buttons outside the identified prompt.</li>
              <li>No, Cancel and Never buttons.</li>
              <li>Hidden or disabled confirmation buttons.</li>
              <li>Dialogs without matching prompt text.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Important:</strong>
              The matcher identifies candidates but never clicks.
              The detector owns the final interaction decision.
            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="testing" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">07 / Testing</span>

            <h2>Tested behaviour.</h2>

            <p>
              Vitest and jsdom provide a simulated DOM environment
              for testing detection behaviour and lifecycle management.
            </p>

            <h3>Run the test suite</h3>

            <pre><code>npm test</code></pre>

            <h3>Watch mode</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run test:watch</code></pre>

            <h3>Coverage areas</h3>

            <ul>
              <li>Prompt detection on YouTube and YouTube Music fixtures.</li>
              <li>Platform-specific dialog selector matching.</li>
              <li>Correct confirmation button identification.</li>
              <li>Unrelated dialogs and buttons ignored.</li>
              <li>Missing, hidden and disabled confirmation buttons.</li>
              <li>Dynamically inserted dialogs.</li>
              <li>Dialog removal and recreation.</li>
              <li>Duplicate click prevention.</li>
              <li>Detector start and stop lifecycle.</li>
              <li>Observer and timer cleanup.</li>
              <li>Polling fallback.</li>
              <li>YouTube SPA navigation handling.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="docs-highlight">

              <span class="eyebrow">Test results</span>

              <h3>26 automated tests pass.</h3>

              <p>
                Simulated DOM fixtures verify detector behaviour
                against recreated prompt structures. They do not
                establish compatibility with YouTube's current
                live interface.
              </p>

            </div>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Verification boundary:</strong>
              Automated tests validate the implementation against
              controlled fixtures. Manual verification is still
              required to confirm genuine YouTube compatibility.
            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="debugging" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">08 / Debugging</span>

            <h2>Inspect what's happening.</h2>

            <p>
              YesYouTube includes an optional debugging interface
              that helps developers inspect detector behaviour.
            </p>

            <h3>Create a debug build</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run build:debug</code></pre>

            <p>
              Reload the extension after generating the debug build.
              Open YouTube and launch your browser's developer tools.
            </p>

            <h3>Console logging</h3>

            <p>
              Debug messages use the following prefix:
            </p>

            <pre><code>[yesYouTube]</code></pre>

            <p>
              When the inactivity prompt is detected, the console
              reports detection and confirmation-click events.
            </p>

            <h3>Debugging interface</h3>

            <p>
              Debug builds expose window.youtubeAutoContinue
              for inspecting and controlling the detector.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-debug-list">

              <div class="docs-debug-item">
                <code>window.youtubeAutoContinue.isRunning</code>
                <span>Check whether detection is running.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-debug-item">
                <code>window.youtubeAutoContinue.stop()</code>
                <span>Stop detection.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-debug-item">
                <code>window.youtubeAutoContinue.start()</code>
                <span>Resume detection.</span>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-debug-item">
                <code>window.youtubeAutoContinue.scan()</code>
                <span>Request one immediate scan.</span>
              </div>

            </div>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Important:</strong>
              scan() does nothing while detection is stopped.
              Call start() before requesting a scan.
            </div>

            <h3>Production versus debug</h3>

            <p>
              The debugging interface and diagnostic logging
              are controlled by the build-time constant
              __YESYOUTUBE_DEBUG__.
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Production builds do not expose the debug interface.</li>
              <li>Production builds omit debug logging.</li>
              <li>Debug builds include diagnostic functionality.</li>
            </ul>

          </section>

          <section id="verification" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">09 / Manual verification</span>

            <h2>Test the real experience.</h2>

            <p>
              Automated tests use simulated DOM fixtures.
              Manual verification is necessary to establish
              compatibility with YouTube's genuine interface.
            </p>

            <h3>Before testing</h3>

            <ul>
              <li>Build the extension using the debug configuration.</li>
              <li>Load the generated dist/ directory.</li>
              <li>Open a YouTube tab in the foreground.</li>
              <li>Enable Autoplay.</li>
              <li>Open browser developer tools.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Test on YouTube</h3>

            <div class="docs-verification-list">

              <div class="docs-verification-item">
                <span>01</span>
                <p>
                  Open <code>https://www.youtube.com/</code>
                  and start watching a video.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-verification-item">
                <span>02</span>
                <p>
                  Leave the tab untouched and wait for the
                  inactivity confirmation prompt.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-verification-item">
                <span>03</span>
                <p>
                  Inspect the developer console for
                  <code>[yesYouTube]</code> messages.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-verification-item">
                <span>04</span>
                <p>
                  Confirm that the expected confirmation
                  button is clicked.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="docs-verification-item">
                <span>05</span>
                <p>
                  Check whether playback resumes.
                </p>
              </div>

            </div>

            <h3>Repeat on YouTube Music</h3>

            <pre><code>https://music.youtube.com/</code></pre>

            <p>
              Repeat the verification process to check the
              YouTube Music dialog structure independently.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Timing note:</strong>
              The documented web inactivity prompt generally
              appears after 60 minutes of uninterrupted watching.
              Actual behaviour may vary with YouTube's implementation.
            </div>

          </section>

          <section id="troubleshooting" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">10 / Troubleshooting</span>

            <h2>Something not working?</h2>

            <p>
              Use the following workflow when the detector
              stops identifying the inactivity confirmation prompt.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-troubleshooting-list">

              <article class="docs-troubleshooting-item">
                <span class="eyebrow">Problem 01</span>
                <h3>Prompt is not detected.</h3>
                <p>
                  Create a debug build, reload the extension
                  and inspect the actual dialog structure
                  using browser developer tools.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-troubleshooting-item">
                <span class="eyebrow">Problem 02</span>
                <h3>Button is not identified.</h3>
                <p>
                  Inspect the confirmation button's accessible
                  label, visibility and disabled state.
                  Compare them with the configured labels.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-troubleshooting-item">
                <span class="eyebrow">Problem 03</span>
                <h3>YouTube changed its interface.</h3>
                <p>
                  Review the dialog selectors and prompt patterns
                  in src/content/config.ts.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-troubleshooting-item">
                <span class="eyebrow">Problem 04</span>
                <h3>Playback does not resume.</h3>
                <p>
                  Verify that the confirmation click occurred.
                  Other YouTube playback conditions may still
                  prevent playback from continuing.
                </p>
              </article>

            </div>

            <h3>Recommended maintenance workflow</h3>

            <pre><code>npm run build:debug

# Inspect the dialog and update config.ts

npm run typecheck
npm test
npm run build</code></pre>

            <p>
              When changing detection rules, add or update
              a simulated DOM fixture and its corresponding test.
            </p>

          </section>

          <section id="limitations" class="docs-section">

            <span class="eyebrow">11 / Known limitations</span>

            <h2>Built with realistic expectations.</h2>

            <p>
              YesYouTube is intentionally small and focused.
              There are a few important boundaries to understand.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-limitations-list">

              <article class="docs-limitation-item">
                <h3>Playback is not guaranteed.</h3>
                <p>
                  The extension attempts to confirm the
                  inactivity prompt. YouTube may still pause
                  playback because of other conditions.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-limitation-item">
                <h3>Interface changes can affect detection.</h3>
                <p>
                  Changes to YouTube's dialog structure,
                  selectors or accessible labels may require
                  configuration updates.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-limitation-item">
                <h3>Simulated tests are not live-site tests.</h3>
                <p>
                  Automated tests verify behaviour against
                  recreated DOM fixtures, not the current
                  production YouTube interface.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="docs-limitation-item">
                <h3>Supported websites are limited.</h3>
                <p>
                  Content scripts are configured for
                  www.youtube.com and music.youtube.com.
                </p>
              </article>

            </div>

            <div class="docs-notice">
              <strong>Project philosophy:</strong>
              YesYouTube aims to handle one specific interruption
              safely, without unnecessary permissions,
              background services or network communication.
            </div>

          </section>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/docs-navigation';
  import '../../scripts/docs-code';
</script>
